      "kv_import_ms": 0.0,
      "text": " The null hypothesis states that the observed percentages of obese individuals in each age class are equal to the expected percentages from the national average. The alternative hypothesis suggests that there is a difference between the observed and expected percentages. The chi-square (χ2) test statistic is calculated by comparing the observed and expected frequencies in each category. The degrees of freedom are calculated as the number of categories minus one. The critical value of the chi-square distribution is obtained from the chi-square distribution table for the desired significance level (in this case, 5%). The decision to reject or fail to reject the null hypothesis is based on whether the calculated test statistic is greater than the critical value. The conclusion is that the observed data significantly deviate from the expected data, indicating that the survey participants are not a representative sample of the USA obese population."
